    "referenceEn": "For decades, clearing urban slums often meant relocating families to distant outskirts completely severed from their existing workplaces. Consequently, low-income residents lost daily earnings and drifted back to build makeshift shelters near downtown, repeating the cycle of poverty and poor sanitation. Learning from past failures, progressive cities now embrace on-site upgrading designed alongside local residents. Rather than trying to remove entire neighborhoods overnight, authorities upgrade water supply lines, build neighborhood health services, and extend commuter railroads linking directly to job hubs. When families gain secure addresses, reliable healthcare, and access to a decent living wage, the local crime rate drops substantially. Sustainable urban planning does not push vulnerable groups out of sight; it builds shared infrastructure where every citizen can thrive.",
